+# `evlog report --since`
+
+## What changed
+
+- **`evlog/cli.py`**: `report` takes `--since DATE`. `parse_since` accepts a bare date (`2024-03-10`, meaning midnight UTC) or an ISO 8601 timestamp; invalid values produce an argparse usage error (exit 2, no traceback). Events before the cutoff are filtered out before counting. A missing or unreadable events file now prints `evlog: cannot read ...` to stderr and exits 1 instead of raising a traceback.
+- **`evlog/events.py`**: added `to_utc` and `since`. `parse_line` now stores every timestamp as aware UTC. Naive timestamps (legacy collectors, per the README) are treated as UTC. Before this, naive and offset timestamps could not be compared.
+- **`tests/test_since.py`** (new): 14 tests covering `parse_since`, timestamp normalisation, boundary and offset cutoffs, zero matches, bad `--since` values, a missing `--since` value, and a missing file. I left the teammate's `tests/test_report.py` unchanged.
+- **`README.md`**: documented `--since`.
+
+## What I verified
+
+- `python -m unittest -v` (run from the project root): **16 tests, all OK**. This includes the teammate's `test_since_excludes_older_days`, which failed before the change because `--since` did not exist.
+- Manual runs against a 3-line sample file:
+  - `python -m evlog report /tmp/bb_events.jsonl --since 2024-03-10` printed `api error 1`, `worker info 1`, `total 2`. The naive `2024-03-09 22:10:00` event was correctly excluded.
+  - `--since 2024-02-30` exited 2 with `invalid date '2024-02-30': expected YYYY-MM-DD or an ISO 8601 timestamp`.
+  - `--since` with no value exited 2 with `expected one argument`.
+  - A missing file exited 1 with `evlog: cannot read /tmp/nope.jsonl: No such file or directory`.
+
+## Not verified / open points
+
+- **Timezone choice.** I interpreted a bare `--since DATE` as midnight UTC, matching the README's rule for naive timestamps. Ops may expect midnight in some local zone; they can pass an explicit offset (e.g. `2024-03-10T00:00:00+09:00`) if so. Please confirm this is the intended reading.
+- **Malformed event lines are still a traceback.** A bad JSON line, or one missing `ts`/`service`/`level`, still raises an uncaught exception. That is file content rather than command-line input, so I left it alone.
+- **Only Python 3.14 was tested.** `datetime.fromisoformat` accepts more formats on newer Pythons, so the set of accepted `--since` values may differ on older versions.
+- I did not run a linter or type checker; none is configured in the repo.
